/-
# `Work.SmallRank` : FT 3.4 (Blackburn's theorem) in the form consumed by FT 8.16 and 26.1–26.2

Area **P2-02** of the Feit–Thompson drive.  References: W. Feit, J. G. Thompson,
*Solvability of groups of odd order*, Pacific J. Math. **13** (1963), 775–1029 (cited as FT below):

* FT 3.4 (Ch. I §3, p. 781) — Blackburn's classification of the `p`-groups all of whose normal
  abelian subgroups are generated by two elements.  Source text: `Submission/Work/ft1963/tex/ch1.md`.
* FT 8.16, 8.17 (Ch. II §8, pp. 794–795) — the `p'`-automorphism analysis of a `p`-group with a
  subgroup `P₀` of order `p` with cyclic `C(P₀)/P₀`.  Source text: `.../tex/ch2.md`.
* FT 26.1, 26.2 (Ch. IV §26, pp. 900–901) — the consequence used by the maximal subgroup analysis.

## The statement being formalised

**3.4.** *Let `p` be an odd prime and `X` a `p`-group.  If every normal abelian subgroup of `X` is
cyclic, then `X` is cyclic* (`Work.SmallRank.blackburn_cyclic`).  *If every normal abelian
subgroup of `X` is generated by two elements, then `X` is isomorphic to one of* (i) *a central
product of a cyclic group and the non abelian group of order `p³` and exponent `p`*, (ii) *a
metacyclic group*, (iii) *the exceptional family*
`gp<A,B | [B,A] = C, [C,A] = B^{r p^{n-1}}, C^p = [B,C] = A^p = B^{p^n} = 1, n > 1, (r,p) = 1>`, (iv)
*a 3-group*.

## What this area delivers (see `NOTES.md` for the full account)

The paper uses 3.4 only through its consequence "for `p ≥ 5` and `P` with no elementary abelian
subgroup of order `p³`: either `P` is metacyclic, or `Ω₁(P)` is the non abelian group of order `p³`
and exponent `p` and `P/Ω₁(P)` is cyclic" (FT 26.1, 26.2, and the branch of 8.16 that cites 3.4).
That dichotomy — `Work.SmallRank.blackburn_weak` — is what this area proves, following Huppert,
*Endliche Gruppen I*, III §§9–12 (P. Hall's regular `p`-groups), not the literal FT 3.4 (whose
exceptional case (iii) no consumer needs).  FT 3.4's *first* sentence, `blackburn_cyclic`, is
proved unconditioanlly.

## Conventions

* All predicates of this area are **type level** (`(X : Type*) [Group X]`), because FT 3.4 is a
  statement about the isomorphism type of a group; subgroup-level versions are obtained with `↥P`.
* `Z(E) = Work.PGroup.centerIn E`, `Ω₁(X) = Work.PGroup.omega p 1 ⊤`, `[A,B] = ⁅A,B⁆`,
  `℧¹(H) = Work.PGroup.agemo p 1 H`.
* Metacyclic groups are P2-01's `Work.LocalPrelim.IsMetacyclicGroup`; `IsMetacyclic` below is a
  transparent alias so that callers can use `Work.SmallRank.IsMetacyclic` uniformly.  (P2-01's
  definition is `∃ C : Subgroup X, C.Normal ∧ IsCyclic ↥C ∧ ∃ x, C ⊔ Subgroup.zpowers x = ⊤`,
  i.e. `X = C⟨x⟩` with `C` cyclic normal — equivalently `X/C` cyclic.)
-/
import Mathlib
import Submission.Work.PGroup.Basic
import Submission.Work.LocalPrelim.Defs

-- ftport:v435-opts-begin
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency false
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency.instanceSearchTypes false
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency.types false
-- ftport:v435-opts-end

set_option linter.unusedVariables false

open scoped Classical Pointwise commutatorElement
open Subgroup

noncomputable section

namespace Work.SmallRank

variable {G : Type*} [Group G]

/-! ### FT 3.4 (i): the central product of a cyclic group and the non abelian group of order `p³` -/

/-- The non abelian group of order `p ^ 3` and exponent `p` (FT 3.4 (i)): a group of order `p ^ 3`
all of whose elements have order dividing `p` and which is not abelian.  (For `p` prime these
conditions determine the isomorphism type; for `p = 2` there is no such group.) -/
def IsGroupOfOrderP3ExpP (p : ℕ) (X : Type*) [Group X] : Prop :=
  Nat.card X = p ^ 3 ∧ (∀ x : X, x ^ p = 1) ∧ ¬ IsMulCommutative X

/-- FT 3.4 (i): `X` is a central product of a cyclic group and the non abelian group of order
`p ^ 3` and exponent `p`.  The two factors are required to meet exactly in the centre of the
second factor: the central product is *proper*, i.e. not a direct product.  (The direct product
`C_p × E` contains the normal elementary abelian subgroup `⟨c, x, z⟩` of order `p ^ 3`, so it is
correctly excluded here.) -/
def IsCentralProductCyclicExtraspecialP3 (p : ℕ) (X : Type*) [Group X] : Prop :=
  ∃ C E : Subgroup X, IsCyclic ↥C ∧ IsGroupOfOrderP3ExpP p ↥E ∧
    C ⊔ E = ⊤ ∧ ⁅C, E⁆ = ⊥ ∧ C ⊓ E = Work.PGroup.centerIn E

/-! ### FT 3.4 (ii): metacyclic groups -/

/-- FT 3.4 (ii): a metacyclic group, i.e. P2-01's `Work.LocalPrelim.IsMetacyclicGroup`. -/
abbrev IsMetacyclic (X : Type*) [Group X] : Prop := Work.LocalPrelim.IsMetacyclicGroup X

/-! ### The rank hypothesis and `Ω₁` -/

/-- `X` has no elementary abelian subgroup of order `p ^ 3` at all (FT: "G does not contain an
elementary subgroup of order `p³`"; the defining condition 2 of FT's `π₂`).  `Work.PGroup`
delivered FT 8.4 (i), which shows that for a `p`-group this is equivalent to "every abelian
subgroup is generated by two elements". -/
def NoElementaryP3 (p : ℕ) (X : Type*) [Group X] : Prop :=
  ¬ ∃ E : Subgroup X, Work.PGroup.IsElementaryAbelian p E ∧ Nat.card E = p ^ 3

/-- `Ω₁(X)`, the subgroup generated by the elements of order dividing `p` (FT §2). -/
def Omega1 (p : ℕ) (X : Type*) [Group X] : Subgroup X := Work.PGroup.omega p 1 ⊤

/-- FT 8.16 (iii) / 26.1: `Ω₁(X)` is the non abelian group of order `p³` and exponent `p`. -/
def Omega1NonabelianP3 (p : ℕ) (X : Type*) [Group X] : Prop :=
  IsGroupOfOrderP3ExpP p ↥(Omega1 p X)

/-- `X / Ω₁(X)` is cyclic, spelled without the quotient group (which needs `Omega1 p X` to be
normal as an instance): `X = Ω₁(X)⟨x⟩` for some `x`.  This is the second half of the
conclusion of FT 3.4 in the form consumed by FT 26.2. -/
def IsCyclicModOmega1 (p : ℕ) (X : Type*) [Group X] : Prop :=
  ∃ x : X, Omega1 p X ⊔ Subgroup.zpowers x = ⊤

end Work.SmallRank
